ther. Actual proper-name capitalization and whole-name boundaries required. Explicit occurrence-level ambiguity exclusions override this rule.","sourceHeadwordId":"Elijah","term":"Elijah"}],"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","matchingRules":["reviewed_exact_headword"],"sourceProvenanceId":"Elijah","sourceTitle":"Elijah"},{"evidence":[{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"“For behold, the day is coming, burning like a furnace, when all the arrogant and every evildoer will be stubble; the day is coming when I will set them ablaze,” says the LORD of Hosts. “Not a root or branch will be left to them.”","verse":1},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"Then you will trample the wicked, for they will be ashes under the soles of your feet on the day I am preparing,” says the LORD of Hosts.","verse":3},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"Behold, I will send you Elijah the prophet before the coming of the great and awesome Day of the LORD.","verse":5}],"id":"Lord","label":"LORD","matchReviews":[{"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","scope":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","senseReview":"Only literal uppercase LORD is admitted globally. Source Lord article explicitly covers God as Lord; lowercase human lord and sentence-title Lord are not inferred from this rule.","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord","term":"LORD"}],"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","matchingRules":["reviewed_exact_headword"],"sourceProvenanceId":"Lord","sourceTitle":"Lord"},{"evidence":[{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"Moses","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_unambiguous_name","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Moses::Moses::reviewed_unambiguous_name","sourceHeadwordId":"Moses"},"text":"“Remember the law of My servant Moses, the statutes and ordinances I commanded him for all Israel at Horeb.","verse":4}],"id":"Moses","label":"Moses","matchReviews":[{"ruleId":"Moses::Moses::reviewed_unambiguous_name","scope":"reviewed_unambiguous_name","senseReview":"Reviewed complete original name headword and source subject. Article covers the biblical person(s), named place, or named spiritual figure denoted by this exact spelling; multiple persons under one unchanged source article remain together. Actual proper-name capitalization and whole-name boundaries required. Explicit occurrence-level ambiguity exclusions override this rule.","sourceHeadwordId":"Moses","term":"Moses"}],"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","matchingRules":["reviewed_exact_headword"],"sourceProvenanceId":"Moses","sourceTitle":"Moses"}],"format":"bsb-study-chapter-v2","notes":[{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Mal.3.13-4.3","r":"Mal.3.13-4.3","rawR":"Mal.3.13-4.3","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.3.13-4.3","value":"Mal.3.13-4.3"}],"t":"ref","v":"3:13–4:3"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," Malachi’s final message contains two distinct but related speeches, the first emphasizing service to the Lord (",{"href":"?bref=Mal.3.13-18","r":"Mal.3.13-18","rawR":"Mal.3.13-18","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.3.13-18","value":"Mal.3.13-18"}],"t":"ref","v":"3:13-18"},") and the second contrasting the fate of the wicked with that of the righteous (",{"href":"?bref=Mal.4.1-3","r":"Mal.4.1-3","rawR":"Mal.4.1-3","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.4.1-3","value":"Mal.4.1-3"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:1-3"},"). Each speech concludes with the messenger formula (see ",{"href":"?item=Mal.1.8_StudyNote_Filament","id":"Mal.1.8_StudyNote_Filament","rawId":"Mal.1.8_StudyNote_Filament","t":"link","v":"study note on 1:8"},"). The prophet revisits themes from the fourth message (",{"href":"?bref=Mal.3.1-5","r":"Mal.3.1-5","rawR":"Mal.3.1-5","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.3.1-5","value":"Mal.3.1-5"}],"t":"ref","v":"3:1-5"},") as he reiterates God’s desire for honesty and faithfulness in worship in view of coming judgment on the day of the Lord. If Malachi is a courtroom drama, this last disputation is the sentencing. While wickedness seems to triumph over righteousness and God seems delinquent in judging sin in the community, the coming day of the Lord will vindicate God’s justice as the wicked are separated from the righteous by the fire of God’s judgment."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Mal.3.13-4.3","refs":[{"book":"MAL","endChapter":4,"endVerse":3,"startChapter":3,"startVerse":13}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Mal.3.13-4.3","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"MAL","sourceChapterCoverage":[3,4],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"5625b95a16f12722902e7edc19dc6f5f05e34099587618d99090614f7fb2f92d","sourceItemName":"Mal.3.13-4.3","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":11709,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"MAL","canonicalOSIS":"Mal","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Mal"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Mal.3.13-4.3","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Mal.3.13-4.3","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Mal.4.1-3","r":"Mal.4.1-3","rawR":"Mal.4.1-3","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.4.1-3","value":"Mal.4.1-3"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:1-3"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," This message shifts away from the disputation format, directly warning the people that repentance is the only proper response to the Lord’s message because God’s judgment is inescapable."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Mal.4.1-3","refs":[{"book":"MAL","endChapter":4,"endVerse":3,"startChapter":4,"startVerse":1}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Mal.4.1-3","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"MAL","sourceChapterCoverage":[4],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"14499985eb04fc2aa7e327041aca0638e593007339848f7bbd07f1e4b4687703","sourceItemName":"Mal.4.1-3","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":11716,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"MAL","canonicalOSIS":"Mal","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Mal"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Mal.4.1-3","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Mal.4.1-3","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Mal.4.2","r":"Mal.4.2","rawR":"Mal.4.2","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.4.2","value":"Mal.4.2"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:2"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," The source for the title ",{"c":["Sun of Righteousness"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," might have been the winged sun disk that is ubiquitous in ancient Near Eastern iconography. Here it might be a title for the Messiah or a figurative description of a new era of righteousness in which God will overturn the curse of sin. Israel’s spiritual restoration, or ",{"c":["healing"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"},{"c":[","],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," would be based on God’s cleansing the people and forgiving their sins (see ",{"href":"?bref=Jer.33.6-8","r":"Jer.33.6-8","rawR":"Jer.33.6-8","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Jer.33.6-8","value":"Jer.33.6-8"}],"t":"ref","v":"Jer 33:6-8"},"; cp. ",{"href":"?bref=Jer.8.14-15","r":"Jer.8.14-15","rawR":"Jer.8.14-15","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Jer.8.14-15","value":"Jer.8.14-15"}],"t":"ref","v":"Jer 8:14-15"},"). It would result from a collective confession of sin and their turning back to God (",{"href":"?bref=Mal.3.7","r":"Mal.3.7","rawR":"Mal.3.7","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.3.7","value":"Mal.3.7"}],"t":"ref","v":"Mal 3:7"},"; cp. ",{"href":"?bref=Jer.14.19-20","r":"Jer.14.19-20","rawR":"Jer.14.19-20","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Jer.14.19-20","value":"Jer.14.19-20"}],"t":"ref","v":"Jer 14:19-20"},"). • ",{"c":["in his wings:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," Outstretched wings are a symbol of God’s protection and rescue (see ",{"href":"?bref=Exod.19.4","r":"Exod.19.4","rawR":"Exod.19.4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Exod.19.4","value":"Exod.19.4"}],"t":"ref","v":"Exod 19:4"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Deut.32.10-11","r":"Deut.32.10-11","rawR":"Deut.32.10-11","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.32.10-11","value":"Deut.32.10-11"}],"t":"ref","v":"Deut 32:10-11"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Ps.17.8","r":"Ps.17.8","rawR":"Ps.17.8","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.17.8","value":"Ps.17.8"}],"t":"ref","v":"Ps 17:8"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Ps.18.10","r":"Ps.18.10","rawR":"Ps.18.10","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.18.10","value":"Ps.18.10"}],"t":"ref","v":"18:10"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Mal.4.2","refs":[{"book":"MAL","endChapter":4,"endVerse":2,"startChapter":4,"startVerse":2}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Mal.4.2","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"MAL","sourceChapterCoverage":[4],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"b0c02347add950853cd733d618ad3ae07b771b23202f747ee18992ae0ea7a944","sourceItemName":"Mal.4.2","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":11717,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"MAL","canonicalOSIS":"Mal","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Mal"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Mal.4.2","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Mal.4.2","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Mal.4.4-6","r":"Mal.4.4-6","rawR":"Mal.4.4-6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.4.4-6","value":"Mal.4.4-6"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:4-6"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," The book concludes with an epilogue containing appeals to Moses and Elijah, two ideal models of faith in the Lord and of the ideals of the Law and Prophets (see ",{"href":"?bref=Matt.17.1-4","r":"Matt.17.1-4","rawR":"Matt.17.1-4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Matt.17.1-4","value":"Matt.17.1-4"}],"t":"ref","v":"Matt 17:1-4"},"). The two are upheld as examples for Malachi’s audience to follow. In ancient Hebrew tradition, the two appeals may have served as postscripts for the scroll that contained the twelve Minor (shorter) Prophets. If so, the first postscript (",{"href":"?bref=Mal.4.4","r":"Mal.4.4","rawR":"Mal.4.4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.4.4","value":"Mal.4.4"}],"t":"ref","v":"Mal 4:4"},") connects the scroll to the Law of Moses. The second (",{"href":"?bref=Mal.4.5-6","r":"Mal.4.5-6","rawR":"Mal.4.5-6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.4.5-6","value":"Mal.4.5-6"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:5-6"},") ties the scroll of the Minor Prophets to the Major (longer) Prophets—Isaiah, Jeremiah, and Ezekiel."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Mal.4.4-6","refs":[{"book":"MAL","endChapter":4,"endVerse":6,"startChapter":4,"startVerse":4}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Mal.4.4-6","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"MAL","sourceChapterCoverage":[4],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"90080f71170196c7426e38ce24453a56a658a26968cb04326c4db06d8f791eb4","sourceItemName":"Mal.4.4-6","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":11718,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"MAL","canonicalOSIS":"Mal","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Mal"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Mal.4.4-6","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Mal.4.4-6","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Mal.4.4","r":"Mal.4.4","rawR":"Mal.4.4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.4.4","value":"Mal.4.4"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:4"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," The first postscript reminds Judah ",{"c":["to obey the Law of Moses"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"},". Israel’s identity was rooted in the Exodus and defined by the Sinai covenant mediated by Moses (see ",{"href":"?bref=Deut.34.10-12","r":"Deut.34.10-12","rawR":"Deut.34.10-12","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.34.10-12","value":"Deut.34.10-12"}],"t":"ref","v":"Deut 34:10-12"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Mal.4.4","refs":[{"book":"MAL","endChapter":4,"endVerse":4,"startChapter":4,"startVerse":4}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Mal.4.4","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"MAL","sourceChapterCoverage":[4],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"6deb4d472ea927c5d40110216828e303660b26542cc6a44a1c67b5b38cbf8419","sourceItemName":"Mal.4.4","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":11719,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"MAL","canonicalOSIS":"Mal","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Mal"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Mal.4.4","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Mal.4.4","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Mal.4.5-6","r":"Mal.4.5-6","rawR":"Mal.4.5-6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.4.5-6","value":"Mal.4.5-6"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:5-6"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," The second postscript warns that divine judgment of the wicked is indeed approaching, and promises divine deliverance and restoration of the righteous. • ",{"c":["Elijah"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," is a supreme example of a prophet of God who preached repentance with messages that were authenticated by signs and wonders (see ",{"href":"?bref=Luke.1.17","r":"Luke.1.17","rawR":"Luke.1.17","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Luke.1.17","value":"Luke.1.17"}],"t":"ref","v":"Luke 1:17"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Jas.5.17-18","r":"Jas.5.17-18","rawR":"Jas.5.17-18","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Jas.5.17-18","value":"Jas.5.17-18"}],"t":"ref","v":"Jas 5:17-18"},"). Elijah was a forerunner of the ",{"c":["day of the"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["Lord"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt-divine-name","t":"span"}," (see ",{"href":"?bref=Mal.3.1","r":"Mal.3.1","rawR":"Mal.3.1","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.3.1","value":"Mal.3.1"}],"t":"ref","v":"Mal 3:1"},"). The New Testament identifies John the Baptist as the prophet who prepared the way for Jesus the Messiah (see ",{"href":"?bref=Matt.11.11-15","r":"Matt.11.11-15","rawR":"Matt.11.11-15","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Matt.11.11-15","value":"Matt.11.11-15"}],"t":"ref","v":"Matt 11:11-15"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Luke.1.17","r":"Luke.1.17","rawR":"Luke.1.17","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Luke.1.17","value":"Luke.1.17"}],"t":"ref","v":"Luke 1:17"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Mal.4.5-6","refs":[{"book":"MAL","endChapter":4,"endVerse":6,"startChapter":4,"startVerse":5}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Mal.4.5-6","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"MAL","sourceChapterCoverage":[4],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"a1cc8e2e0ff4365fd71b76686db81194395e865b502a4ce2dde26515ab2c2761","sourceItemName":"Mal.4.5-6","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":11720,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"MAL","canonicalOSIS":"Mal","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Mal"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Mal.4.5-6","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Mal.4.5-6","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Mal.4.6","r":"Mal.4.6","rawR":"Mal.4.6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.4.6","value":"Mal.4.6"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:6"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["His preaching will turn the hearts of fathers to their children:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," Two key themes prominent in Malachi and the Old Testament prophets in general are the turning of hearts and the ministry of reconciliation. ",{"c":["Turn"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," is the Old Testament term for repentance and indicates a complete change of loyalties. Turning toward God results in reconciliation between generations (see ",{"href":"?bref=2Cor.5.18-20","r":"2Cor.5.18-20","rawR":"2Cor.5.18-20","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Cor.5.18-20","value":"2Cor.5.18-20"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Cor 5:18-20"},"). • ",{"c":["strike the land with a curse:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," The word ",{"c":["curse"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," implies total destruction (see ",{"href":"?bref=Deut.7.26","r":"Deut.7.26","rawR":"Deut.7.26","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.7.26","value":"Deut.7.26"}],"t":"ref","v":"Deut 7:26"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=1Sam.15.18","r":"1Sam.15.18","rawR":"1Sam.15.18","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Sam.15.18","value":"1Sam.15.18"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Sam 15:18"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Zech.14.11","r":"Zech.14.11","rawR":"Zech.14.11","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Zech.14.11","value":"Zech.14.11"}],"t":"ref","v":"Zech 14:11"},"). The people of Israel who did not respond to God’s prophet would face utter oblivion, as had been the fate of their Canaanite predecessors (e.g., ",{"href":"?bref=Josh.6.17","r":"Josh.6.17","rawR":"Josh.6.17","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Josh.6.17","value":"Josh.6.17"}],"t":"ref","v":"Josh 6:17"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Mal.4.6","refs":[{"book":"MAL","endChapter":4,"endVerse":6,"startChapter":4,"startVerse":6}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Mal.4.6","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"MAL","sourceChapterCoverage":[4],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"9f00f98a917cff50228e70935b4c04e89f04c608f3d0e3ec053c65443e2dfa8e","sourceItemName":"Mal.4.6","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":11721,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"MAL","canonicalOSIS":"Mal","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Mal"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Mal.4.6","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Mal.4.6","type":"StudyNote"}],"version":"v2-a101d843a2f0cd3fc0c6"}